sc.manual.armor.charge.1=Weapons fire instantly at the target closest to your crosshair, within range and line of sight. Charge them with sneak + right-click on any machine or generator - it takes energy from their buffer.
sc.manual.armor.chip.defense=damage resistance, level = tier
sc.manual.armor.chip.mobility=speed, level = tier
sc.manual.armor.chip.power=strength (melee damage), level = tier; with the Exo helmet's solar film the Power Chip adds +25% of its energy per tier
sc.manual.armor.chip.sensor=night vision
sc.manual.armor.chip.utility=haste (faster mining), level = tier
sc.manual.armor.chips.1=Right-click a chip while wearing a suit chestplate; one chip of each type. A new chip of the same type swaps the old one out and returns it to your inventory. The "Remove chips" button on the chestplate tab of the armour screen (K) takes all of them out at once. The chip's tier (I-III) shows in its tooltip; tiers II and III are made in the Upgrade Stations.
sc.manual.armor.chipshead=Chips
sc.manual.armor.effects.1=Each chip and each function heats the suit; the suit sheds a little heat itself (see above), the chestplate's helium loop takes the rest. The surroundings count too: the Nether +3/s, lava or fire nearby +5/s, a hot biome by day under the sky +1/s; a cold biome -1/s, water -2/s, above y 150 -1/s. Above 80% you are slowed; at 100% the chips shut off (slowness and weakness) until the suit has cooled to half.
sc.manual.armor.energy.1=The armour is electric, like IC2's nano and quantum suits: every piece stores EU and protects only while charged. Each point of absorbed damage costs charge; an empty piece gives no protection at all. There is no durability - the bar under the icon shows the charge.
sc.manual.armor.energyline=%d EU per piece, %d EU per damage point, charges from %s
sc.manual.armor.energy.2=Charge it in an energy storage's slot, with sneak + right-click on a machine or generator, or with IC2 in any of its chargers (storage blocks, charge pads) of the suit's tier or higher. Chips run on the chestplate: 20 EU per second per chip tier; without charge they switch off.
sc.manual.armor.energyhead=Energy
sc.manual.armor.suitline=armour %d (%d%% protection), heat capacity %d, sheds %d/s
sc.manual.armor.suits.1=Four suits from weakest to strongest: Nano, Quantum, Exo and Singular. Nano equals diamond armour; each next one protects better and holds more heat.
sc.manual.armor.title=Armour and weapons
sc.manual.armor.upgrade.1=Nano upgrades to Quantum in the HV Upgrade Station, Quantum to Exo in the EV one (or any higher station: an upgraded station still knows every lower station's recipes). Installed chips, the charge and all settings (power mode, switched functions) are kept. Exo turns into the Singular suit in the Singular Service Station.
sc.manual.armor.weaponline=%d damage x%d, %d EU per shot, range %d, battery %d EU
sc.manual.armor.weapons=Weapons
sc.manual.chain.intro.1=Every step below is a real fab process. Under each description are the machine's recipes that move silicon forward (every recipe is in the Recipes chapter).
sc.manual.chain.outro.1=Dies, transistors, memory and controllers go into the boards and modules of higher-tier machines, armour and weapons. Defect scrap goes back into silica sand in the Crusher.
sc.manual.chain.step.blast_furnace=Carbothermic reduction: sand + coke + limestone flux at ~2000 C give metallurgical-grade silicon (~98%).
sc.manual.chain.step.chem_reactor=Silicon reacts with HCl into trichlorosilane SiHCl3 - a volatile liquid that is easy to purify.
sc.manual.chain.step.crusher=Quartzite is crushed into silica sand - nearly pure SiO2.
sc.manual.chain.step.cvd_chamber=Siemens process: SiHCl3 is decomposed by hydrogen on hot rods, depositing electronic-grade polysilicon.
sc.manual.chain.step.czochralski_puller=Czochralski method: a seed is slowly pulled from the melt under argon, growing a single-crystal ingot (boule).
sc.manual.chain.step.dicing_saw=The finished wafer is cut into individual dies with a diamond blade.
sc.manual.chain.step.etching_bath=Developer removes the exposed resist, hydrofluoric acid etches the uncovered oxide.
sc.manual.chain.step.ion_implanter=Boron, phosphorus or arsenic ions are accelerated into the silicon - this makes the p- and n-regions.
sc.manual.chain.step.oxidation_furnace=Wafers are heated in oxygen to grow an insulating SiO2 layer.
sc.manual.chain.step.packager=The die is mounted on a lead frame, wire-bonded and moulded into a package - a chip.
sc.manual.chain.step.photoresist_coater=The spinner spreads a thin, even film of photoresist over the wafer.
sc.manual.chain.step.sputterer=Magnetron sputtering: argon plasma knocks metal atoms off a target to lay down the wiring.
sc.manual.chain.step.stepper=The stepper projects the photomask pattern with UV light, die by die, under argon.
sc.manual.chain.step.wire_saw=A diamond-coated multi-wire saw slices the boule into thin wafers; water cools it and carries the swarf away.
sc.manual.chain.title=The silicon chain: quartzite to controller
sc.manual.energy.cableline=tier %s: packets up to %d EU, %d EU/t in all (%d A), loss %d EU per block
sc.manual.energy.cables=Cables
sc.manual.energy.bundlehead=Conduit bundles
sc.manual.energy.bundle.1=Conduits work like Ender IO's: a cable, a pipe and a pneumatic tube can share one block, each in its own lane. Right-click a bundle with a conduit item to add it (clicking a machine through the gap next to a bundle works too). Breaking takes out the part you are looking at.
sc.manual.energy.bundle.2=Conduits only join conduits of the same type: copper cable to copper cable, steel pipe to steel pipe. Where one meets a machine, tank or chest there is a dark connector.
sc.manual.energy.bundle.3=Right-click an arm or connector empty-handed to open that side's menu, as in Ender IO: tabs for the bundle's conduits on the right, "Mode < >" at the top. Cable: In/Out, Extract, Insert, Disabled; pipe and tube: Insert, Extract, In/Out, Disabled. Extraction has a redstone mode, a tube also a colour channel and round robin or nearest first; insertion has a priority (a tube also a channel). Connector rim: orange extracts, blue inserts, green both. A wrench or sneak + right-click empty-handed cycles the mode without the menu.
sc.manual.energy.rules.4=A source above the cable's tier burns the cable out - no blast, the blocks around are fine (placing the cable or the source warns you in chat). A network above a machine's tier blows the machine itself up (the blocks around stay, the blast still hurts) - use a transformer or a transformer upgrade. No blast breaks a machine or field generator holding a universal transformer upgrade. The same with IC2.
sc.manual.energy.corrosionsafe=resists corrosive chemicals
sc.manual.energy.intro.1=A cable connects to neighbouring cables, machines and generators (and IC2 blocks when IC2 is installed). A pipe connects to any fluid container, the pneumatic tube to inventories. A generator or storage placed right against a machine with its output face powers it without a cable; if the voltage is higher than the machine takes, nothing flows through direct contact (no explosion).
sc.manual.energy.piperules.1=Pipes of one type form a network holding one fluid. An Extract connector takes fluid out of a tank or machine (redstone permitting), the network delivers it to the tanks on Insert connectors: higher priority first, equal ones in turn. You can see the fluid through the pipe's glass.
sc.manual.energy.piperules.2=Corrosive fluids (see Safety) eat through every pipe except PTFE in about 10 seconds of contact.
sc.manual.energy.piperules.3=A pipe holds a small buffer of fluid (at most its rating per tick). Breaking a pipe or taking it out of a bundle loses that buffer - the rest of the network and the tanks lose nothing.
sc.manual.energy.pipes=Pipes
sc.manual.energy.rules.1=Cables of one type form a network. Its rating (volts x amps) is a per-tick limit for the whole network, line loss included.
sc.manual.energy.rules.2=Energy is shared evenly between consumers, not all to the nearest. The supply pays the line loss: the cable's loss x the blocks from the nearest generator to the machine. The superconductor loses nothing.
sc.manual.energy.rules.3=A storage block joined to a network by both an input and its output face acts as a buffer: it takes what the generators have to spare and gives back when they fall short.
sc.manual.energy.storage.1=Energy comes in on five faces and leaves only through the front (the arrow) - it turns to face you when placed. Chain them front-to-side.
sc.manual.energy.storage.2=The slot on its screen charges weapons and armour of the storage's tier or lower. A broken storage keeps its charge in the item; upgrading it to the next tier at a crafting table, or building a charge pad from it, carries the charge over (as much as the new block holds).
sc.manual.energy.storage.3=Above EV the mod has four tiers of its own: IV (%s EU/t), QV - Quantum (%s EU/t), XV - Exo (%s EU/t) and SV - Singular (%s EU/t). They have their own cables (niobium-titanium, quantum, exo, singular), EV-IV, IV-QV, QV-XV and XV-SV transformers and charge pads. Machines of these tiers: %s; feed the other machines through step-down transformers. With IC2: IV is its tier 5, QV and XV tier 6, SV tier 7.
sc.manual.energy.storagehead=Energy storage
sc.manual.energy.storageline=holds %s EU, outputs %d EU/t
sc.manual.energy.transformerhead=Transformers
sc.manual.energy.transformerline=front %d EU/t, other faces %d EU/t; step-down gives up to %d EU/t, step-up up to %d EU/t
sc.manual.energy.transformer.1=Feed a machine from a network of another tier, like IC2's transformers. The front face (turned to you on placement) is the high-voltage side, the other five the low-voltage side.
sc.manual.energy.transformer.2=Step-down (default): high voltage goes in the front and comes out of the other faces at the low voltage - e.g. an HV net feeds an MV machine. Step-up: low voltage goes in on the sides and out of the front at the high voltage - LV generators feed an HV machine. As with IC2, one packet of the output voltage leaves per tick; for more, use several transformers. Right-click empty-handed or with a wrench switches the mode, sneak + right-click empty-handed (or a wrench's rotate) turns the front. No loss; high voltage on the low side blows the transformer up.
sc.manual.energy.title=Energy and logistics
sc.manual.energy.tube.1=Linked pneumatic tubes move up to 4 items a tick out of blocks on Extract connectors into blocks on Insert connectors of the same colour channel: higher priority first, equal ones in turn or nearest first (a switch on the extract connector). Machine sides are respected: take only products, put only fitting ingredients.
sc.manual.energy.tube.2=A tube connector's menu has slots, as in Ender IO: the extract filter and speed upgrades (on Extract), the insert filter (on Insert). The item filter holds 5 examples, whitelist or blacklist, meta; the advanced one 10 examples plus NBT and ore dictionary. Set it up by right-clicking it in hand. A speed upgrade adds 4 items per move each, up to 64.
sc.manual.field.explosions=An explosion near the field (creeper, TNT, a machine failure) destroys no blocks inside it (%d EU per explosion). Creatures are still hurt.
sc.manual.field.formula=Upkeep: (%d EU x 6 faces x node count + %d EU x range) x shape multiplier + the switched-on protections, every tick.
sc.manual.field.intro.1=The Field Generator (HV) is an energy shield: it keeps hostile mobs out, turns their projectiles back and smothers explosions. Several generators join into a cluster whose shield covers them all.
sc.manual.field.limits=Up to %d nodes per cluster, no more than %d blocks apart on each axis.
sc.manual.field.linkhead=Linking
sc.manual.field.linking.1=With a Field Link Module: right-click one generator, then another - their clusters merge. Sneak + right-click air cancels. The first cluster's master stays master; right-click it for the status screen.
sc.manual.field.modeline=x%s: %d EU/t (1 node, range %d) ... %d EU/t (8 nodes, range %d)
sc.manual.field.modes=Modes and field shapes
sc.manual.field.power.1=Energy can go into any node - it is passed to the master, which pays for the field from its buffer.
sc.manual.field.power.2=The master takes upgrades: the Upgrades tab of its screen has four slots. An energy storage upgrade adds 10,000 EU of buffer (up to 16 count). Each transformer upgrade raises the voltage it takes by one tier (HV -> EV -> IV -> QV -> XV) - for every node of the cluster. A node joining another cluster hands its upgrades to the master (what doesn't fit drops); breaking a generator drops them.
sc.manual.field.projectiles=Arrows, fireballs and potions not fired by a player (mobs, dispensers, and arrows after a chunk reload) bounce back off the field (%d EU per projectile).
sc.manual.field.protect=While powered, hostile mobs inside the field (ghasts, slimes and magma cubes included) are pushed out and take damage.
sc.manual.field.protecthead=Protection
sc.manual.field.range=Set the range (radius) on the master's screen, Zone tab, with the -16 / -1 / +1 / +16 buttons: %d to %d blocks (a new generator starts at %d). Also there: the height (the radius by default; spheres and domes with their own height are stretched or squashed, a prism stops short of the whole world height), an offset of the whole field up to 32 blocks on each axis, the centre (every node, the middle of the cluster, or a point where you stand) and the shape; with Preview on, changes first show as a white dashed outline and apply with a button. The tab's bottom line shows the field's volume and upkeep. Sneak + right-clicking the master with an empty hand also changes mode (not with an item in hand).
sc.manual.field.rangehead=Range
sc.manual.field.shape.box=one box around all nodes, grown by 'range' on every side
sc.manual.field.shape.prism=a wall along the nodes' outer outline (seen from above), grown by 'range', the full height of the world, from bedrock to the sky (Y 0-256) whatever the nodes' height - covers from above too
sc.manual.field.shape.union=a sphere of radius 'range' around every node, merging where they overlap
sc.manual.field.title=Field Generator and clusters
sc.manual.field.visible=A running field shows as a faint, shimmering shell of the chosen colour (it can be hidden); without power it fades out.
sc.manual.generator.combustion=Burns diesel from its tank, and crude oil and other mods' fuels (BuildCraft fuel, ethanol, biodiesel, biofuel) - each gives its own amount. Without a fluid, any furnace fuel from the slot.
sc.manual.generator.fusion_reactor=Needs ignition by external power, a Li-Blanket Module in slot 2 (a new one is taken on ignition only once the last is spent; lasts ~14 h of running) and Deuterium Cells - with no cell it won't ignite.
sc.manual.generator.gas_turbine=Burns hydrogen from the electrolyser.
sc.manual.generator.plasma_generator=Ionised argon from the Air Separator in a plasma channel.
sc.manual.generator.solar_gaas=Gallium arsenide panel: 8 times a silicon one; sky, day / night / rain as for every panel.
sc.manual.generator.solar_si=Open sky only: full output by day, half at night, 60% in the rain. The cells are silicon wafers.
sc.manual.generator.steam_turbine=Boiler steam spins the turbine. Pipe steam in.
sc.manual.intro.about.1=This mod walks the real path of microelectronics: from quartz sand to a finished controller.
sc.manual.intro.about.2=Ore is crushed and washed, silicon is reduced in a blast furnace and purified through chlorosilanes, a single crystal is pulled from the melt, sawn into wafers, oxidised, coated with resist, exposed, etched, doped, metallised and diced into dies that are finally packaged into chips.
sc.manual.intro.about.3=Along the way you build a power grid, a chemical plant and rare-metal processing, reactors up to the Tokamak XV and the Singular Reactor, armour with chips and gases up to the Singular suit, service stations, a shield field and the Ground and Space Bridge.
sc.manual.intro.controls.1=Sneak + right-click a machine with an empty hand twice (within a second) - empty its input tanks; if they are already empty, its output tanks. The fluid is lost; it costs 1 EU per %s mB from the machine's buffer.
sc.manual.intro.controls.2=Sneak + right-click a machine or generator with a weapon or armour piece - charge it from their buffer.
sc.manual.intro.controls.3=Right-click a chip while wearing a suit chestplate - install or swap the chip.
sc.manual.intro.controls.4=Field Link Module: right-click two Field Generators to join them; sneak + right-click air cancels. Sneak + right-click a field master cycles its mode.
sc.manual.intro.controlshead=Controls
sc.manual.intro.flow.1=A generator stores energy in its buffer and every tick sends at most its tier's voltage down the cables. A machine collects incoming energy in its own buffer and spends it while an operation runs.
sc.manual.intro.flow.2=A higher-tier machine can run on lower-tier power - it simply works in bursts as its buffer fills. The reverse is forbidden (see below).
sc.manual.intro.gui.1=Everything that works is on the holo screen: top slots are input, bottom slots output, the bar between them is progress (with NEI a click on it opens the recipes), the status under it. On the right: what the machine does and its icon, animated while it works, and the numbers - progress, energy use and input voltage.
sc.manual.intro.gui.2=Tanks show on the screen at full size only if the machine uses them or they hold something: lamp green - holding fluid, orange - full, red - empty, the percentage underneath. The tall energy gauge is right of the screen, heat under the status (furnaces and precision machines only). Upgrades go into the Upgrades row below the screen, with what they do beside it.
sc.manual.intro.gui.3=The status is coloured: green - working, grey - idle, amber - output full, red - no power or overheated. The plate right of the title is the machine's tier.
sc.manual.intro.gui.4=Hover any tank or gauge for exact numbers.
sc.manual.intro.guihead=Reading a machine screen
sc.manual.intro.power.1=EU is the mod's energy unit (the same as IC2's; IC2 is optional). Each tier is stronger than the one before: %s. The exact voltages are in the table:
sc.manual.intro.start.1=1. Smelt chalcopyrite, cassiterite or galena in a furnace for copper, tin and lead.
sc.manual.intro.start.2=2. One copper ingot makes 4 bare copper cables. Build a Combustion Generator (iron, a combustion chamber of a furnace and iron, bare cable) and fuel it with coal, later with diesel. The Solid Fuel Generator (copper, tin, a copper coil, a combustion chamber) needs no machines either.
sc.manual.intro.start.3=3. The first (LV) machines are iron and bare copper cable. Crushing ore before smelting doubles the yield; the Kiln turns slime into rubber.
sc.manual.intro.start.4=4. The Blast Furnace makes steel from iron and carbon dust. Steel and insulated cable unlock MV machines, titanium and tungsten cable HV, superconductor EV.
sc.manual.intro.start.5=Titanium comes from the Kroll process: titanium concentrate (ilmenite) + carbon dust + chlorine in the Chem Reactor give TiCl4, which the Blast Furnace reduces with magnesium (magnesite -> Crusher -> Kiln -> electrolyser). Chlorine and TiCl4 run fine in steel pipe.
sc.manual.intro.start.6=5. The silicon chain needs quartzite (sand), coke, limestone, hydrogen and chlorine - see the Silicon chapter.
sc.manual.intro.start.7=The Rolling Machine needs a press mould in its input - the mould decides the product (plate, blade, coil, target, lead frame).
sc.manual.intro.start.8=With IC2 installed its copper, crushed ores and rubber fit every recipe here. With NEI installed, clicking a machine's progress arrow shows all its recipes.
sc.manual.intro.starthead=Getting started
sc.manual.intro.tierline=up to %d EU/t, machine buffer %d EU
sc.manual.intro.tiers=Energy and tiers
sc.manual.intro.title=Silicon Age: From Wafer to ExoTech - Engineer's Handbook
sc.manual.intro.warning.1=Energy above a machine's tier destroys it in a blast that hurts and knocks back but breaks no blocks around. A cable fed by a source above its tier burns out without a blast. Never connect a higher-tier generator or cable to a lower-tier machine or cable - use a transformer. New blocks are placed switched off: switch them on once the line is checked.
sc.manual.intro.warninghead=WARNING
sc.manual.machine.air_separator=Cryogenic air separation: oxygen and argon from air, liquid helium from water. The 2nd fluid leaves from the top.
sc.manual.machine.blast_furnace=Steel from iron and carbon, metallurgical silicon, titanium by the Kroll process (TiCl4 + magnesium), alloys (Nb3Sn). Builds up heat.
sc.manual.machine.boiler_lv=2500 mB water into 2000 mB steam; fuel - coal in the input slot or 100 mB diesel in the second tank.
sc.manual.machine.boiler_mv=MV Boiler: the same, twice as fast.
sc.manual.machine.centrifuge=Splits purified ore into dust and rare by-products (chance shown in the recipe).
sc.manual.machine.chem_reactor=Trichlorosilane, HCl, HF, developer, hydrides (AsH3, PH3), BCl3, TiCl4 (titanium chlorination), synthetic crude oil; heavy water out of water by hydrogen exchange (2000 mB of water + 50 mB of hydrogen -> 100 mB of heavy water).
sc.manual.machine.chlor_alkali_electrolyzer=Electrolysis: brine into NaOH and chlorine, halite into hydrogen and deuterium, water with lye (10 mB of NaOH as the electrolyte) into hydrogen and oxygen, heavy water into deuterium and oxygen, fluorine, aluminium and magnesium oxides into metal. The 2nd fluid leaves from the top.
sc.manual.machine.crusher=Jaw crusher: ore into crushed ore (x2), quartzite into sand, coal into carbon dust, scrap back into sand.
sc.manual.machine.cvd_chamber=Chemical vapour deposition: SiHCl3 + hydrogen give electronic-grade polysilicon.
sc.manual.machine.czochralski_puller=Grows single-crystal boules from polysilicon under argon. Builds up heat.
sc.manual.machine.czochralski_puller_ev=Faster EV Czochralski Puller.
sc.manual.machine.dicing_saw=Cuts a metallised wafer into dies with a water-cooled diamond blade.
sc.manual.machine.etching_bath=Develops and etches exposed wafers: developer + HF.
sc.manual.machine.fluid_cell_filler=Fills liquid helium and deuterium into cells (needs an empty bucket).
sc.manual.machine.ion_implanter=Dopes etched wafers with ions (PH3, BCl3 or AsH3). Builds up heat.
sc.manual.machine.kiln=Rubber from slime, heat-resistant rubber, ceramic packages, coke, W-Ti plates, calcining magnesite to MgO.
sc.manual.machine.ore_washer=Washes crushed ore with water to strip the gangue. Needs water (1000 mB per operation).
sc.manual.machine.oxidation_furnace=Grows oxide on wafers in oxygen; also coats dies with HfO2.
sc.manual.machine.packager=Packages dies on lead frames into transistors, memory and controllers.
sc.manual.machine.photoresist_coater=Coats oxidised wafers with photoresist.
sc.manual.machine.refinery=Distils crude oil into diesel, polymers, PTFE, photoresist and rubber; platinum dust catalyst.
sc.manual.machine.rolling_machine=The mould in its slot decides the product - plate, blade, coil, target, lead frame.
sc.manual.machine.sputterer=Metallises wafers from a target (copper, aluminium, tungsten); the target wears out.
sc.manual.machine.stepper=Lithography: exposes wafers through a photomask (kept in an input slot, wears out gradually). Builds up heat.
sc.manual.machine.stepper_ev=Faster EV Stepper.
sc.manual.machine.upgrade_station_ev=EV Upgrade Station: Quantum into Exo, the Fusion Core.
sc.manual.machine.upgrade_station_hv=HV Upgrade Station: tier III chips, Nano suit into Quantum (chips are kept).
sc.manual.machine.upgrade_station_mv=MV Upgrade Station: tier II armour chips.
sc.manual.machine.wire_saw=Slices a boule into 8 wafers at once; needs a diamond wire (wears out) and water.
sc.manual.machines.fusioninfo=ignition %s EU, one Deuterium Cell per %d min
sc.manual.machines.generators=Generators
sc.manual.machines.heat=heat
sc.manual.machines.heathead=Heat
sc.manual.machines.heatrule=Furnaces and precision machines gain 1 heat per working tick and lose 2 per idle tick. At %d the operation pauses until cooled to %d; an operation interrupted by overheating has double defect chance. Heat sinks slow the heating and shed heat while working: with two, long recipes don't overheat.
sc.manual.machines.intro.1=Every machine: tier, consumption, number of recipes and purpose. Internal tanks hold %s mB each. Exact recipes are in the Recipes chapter or NEI.
sc.manual.machines.passive=no fuel
sc.manual.machines.recipes=%d recipes
sc.manual.machines.sides.1=All faces of a machine are equal: ingredients (items and fluids) go in on any side, products come out of any side, energy comes in on any side. The machine pushes nothing out by itself - the conduits decide: a pipe or tube connector on Extract takes the products, one on Insert feeds ingredients in. Or fit an Ejector upgrade.
sc.manual.machines.sides.2=Automation (hoppers, tubes) puts each ingredient into one input slot only, so a machine can't clog up with a single raw material.
sc.manual.machines.sideshead=Block faces
sc.manual.machines.upgradeshead=Machine upgrades
sc.manual.machines.upgrades.1=Below a machine's screen is the Upgrades row: %s upgrade slots, as in IC2. Several upgrades of one kind add up (up to %s count). Overclockers need more energy per tick: the machine's buffer grows to hold two working ticks by itself, but the network may not keep up - add transformer and energy storage upgrades. The Tank Extension upgrade (machines and generators): +%s mB to every tank, up to %s (%s -> %s mB); taken out, no fluid is lost - the tank just takes nothing in until it is down to its new size. The small cross above a tank pours it out for EU from the buffer: 1 EU per %s mB. The machine doesn't push fluid out by itself: a pipe connector on Extract or an Ejector upgrade takes the product.
sc.manual.machines.tierhead=%s machines
sc.manual.machines.title=Machines and generators
sc.manual.machines.topout=2 output fluids
sc.manual.machines.trouble.1="No power" - the buffer is empty: check the generator, the cables and their tier.
sc.manual.machines.trouble.2="Output full" - clear the output slots, or pipe the product away (sneak + right-click twice empties the tanks); the machine checks room for every product and possible scrap in advance.
sc.manual.machines.trouble.3="Overheated" is a pause, not a fault: the machine cools down and carries on by itself.
sc.manual.machines.trouble.4="Idle" with inputs loaded - an ingredient or fluid is missing (hover the tank), or the wrong mould/tool is in the slot.
sc.manual.machines.troublehead=When a machine won't run
sc.manual.machines.vent.1=A machine only accepts fluids its recipes use. Sneak + right-click with an empty hand twice (within a second) empties its input tanks, a second time its output tanks; it costs as the small cross does: 1 EU per %s mB.
sc.manual.machines.vent.2=If a machine has two output fluids, the second leaves through the top face - give it its own pipe.
sc.manual.machines.vent.3=A machine broken with a pickaxe carries the fluids of all four tanks and its buffer charge in the item (shown in its tooltip) and gets them back when placed again. The items in its slots drop next to it.
