sc.manual.bridge.t.lifev=%s s (with 4 stabilisers %s s)
sc.manual.bridge.tanks=The bridge's shared tanks (mB): %s. Each extra gas port +%s%% to the tanks. Opening energy comes only from the capacitors (%s EU each), the hold from the energy port, then the capacitors.
sc.manual.bridge.openhead=Opening
sc.manual.bridge.open.1=Switch the power on, set the target and press "Open". The bridge checks everything at once: build and calibration, cooling, room at the target (a floor and free 3x3x2, 5x5x2 for Space; no lava, no water, not the Void, not someone else's field generator), energy in the capacitors, singular matter and gases. If all is there the vortex appears instantly at both ends, their chunks stay loaded.
sc.manual.bridge.open.2=Players pass the portal; mobs, items and minecarts - with the Mass Compensator. While it is open, helium and argon (and heavy water for Space) are spent every second, energy every tick. If something runs out, the vortex folds %s seconds later; stability under %s%% folds it too. The vortex is never put into blocks and disappears completely.
sc.manual.bridge.cool=After a close the ring cools %s s (%s s with the Ring Cooler) - no new portal while it's hot. There are no other cooldowns.
sc.manual.bridge.coordhead=Own coordinates
sc.manual.bridge.coord.1=Fields X, Y, Z - any point of the world. Y may be left empty ("auto") - the bridge finds the topmost safe floor itself; a given Y opens exactly at that height. "Check place" (1 mB of krypton) answers "Place free (on the ground)", "Place free (in the air) — soft landing" or "No room" with the reason and a hint "Nearest free place" within 16 blocks (32 with the Navigation Computer; at the same height when it can); "Take it" puts its coordinates in.
sc.manual.bridge.coord.2=The target can be saved as a bookmark (8, 32 with the Navigation Computer), renamed and deleted; clicking a bookmark puts it into the fields. A Receiver Beacon near the target makes opening %s%% cheaper.
sc.manual.bridge.coord.3=No floor needed: a bridge end opens on the ground and in the air alike - all that matters is a free volume for the vortex (3×3×2 Ground, 5×5×2 Space): not inside blocks, not in lava or water, not below Y = 1 or above the world's ceiling, not in someone else's field generator. Whoever comes out of an end in the air gets a soft landing - a slow fall without damage until touching the ground (%s s at most). If the Void is below the point, the check warns.
sc.manual.bridge.modhead=Modules
sc.manual.bridge.mod.1=Navigation Computer - 32 bookmarks and place search within 32; Mass Compensator - lets mobs, items, minecarts through; Ring Cooler - cools three times faster for helium; Portal Shield - hostile mobs from the far side don't come through.
sc.manual.bridge.wearhead=Ring wear and repair
sc.manual.bridge.wear.1=Every opening wears the ring 1-3%% - more if an end is farther than %s blocks or in another dimension, if a lot of mass went through or the ring got red-hot. Up to %s%% wear changes nothing; above that every %s%% of wear takes 1%% of stability, and the ring heats faster.
sc.manual.bridge.wear.2=The "Repair" button in the controller removes all wear at once: %s mB of helium and %s M EU per percent. Replacing a ring coil doesn't remove wear - it only resets the calibration.
sc.manual.bridge.stabhead=Vortex stability
sc.manual.bridge.stab.1=Stability is recalculated every second: 100%% minus %s%% per missing stabiliser, ring wear, passage mass, interference and an argon shortage. The tooltip over the controller's stability gauge shows every factor; stability is shown on the remote, in the armour's "Bridge" tab and in the line on top of the screen.
sc.manual.bridge.stab.2=Below %s%% the vortex shakes and crackles: the exit shifts up to %s blocks to the nearest place one can stand, travellers are knocked back a little. Below %s%% the vortex folds - whoever was in it stays at the entrance unharmed.
sc.manual.bridge.stab.3=Passage mass: player %s, mob %s, item %s, minecart %s. Every unit costs %s M EU and takes %s%% of stability for %s seconds; the Mass Compensator halves both.
sc.manual.bridge.stab.4=Interference: another bridge controller within %s blocks - minus %s%% stability for both; a thunderstorm at either end - minus %s%%. A running Singular reactor within %s blocks of the ring resonates with it: opening costs %s%% less energy.
sc.manual.bridge.heathead=Heat and overheating
sc.manual.bridge.heat.1=While a portal is open the ring heats up: about %s%% a second, faster for a worn ring, a shaking vortex and without helium. The coils glow blue, then orange and red; a hot ring keeps glowing while it cools.
sc.manual.bridge.heat.2=100%% - emergency shutdown: the portal closes, the ring is locked for %s minutes, wear +%s%%. The world is not damaged. If a hold resource runs out, the vortex folds in %s seconds - the chat and the line on top of the screen warn about it.
sc.manual.bridge.famhead=Familiar places and scatter
sc.manual.bridge.fam.1=The bridge remembers where you have been: chunks you walked holding a coordinator or wearing a Singular helmet with the scanner on, points recorded by a coordinator and the ends of your portals (up to %s chunks per player, the longest forgotten are erased first).
sc.manual.bridge.fam.2=A familiar target is %s%% cheaper and precise. An unfamiliar one costs %s%% more and the exit may shift up to %s blocks (always to a free place); with the Navigation Computer - up to %s blocks, with a Receiver Beacon next to the target or to the scanner's finds from level 5 armour - precise.
sc.manual.bridge.fam.3=Dimension scouting: the first Space bridge trip into a dimension with no Interdimensional Anchor exits with a scatter of up to %s blocks. Its price is already doubled by the missing anchor - it does not grow a second time.
sc.manual.bridge.lookhead=Look
sc.manual.bridge.look.1=The birth of a singularity is a flash and particles flying inward, then the vortex unfolds. While a portal is open a hologram with the target's coordinates hangs above the ring. The vortex is one solid spiral disc with a living rim: green for the Ground bridge, blue-violet with stars for the Space one; as stability drops it turns yellow, at a dangerous level orange with cracks; an end with no ring round it has a sparking energy rim.
